                "General - START Help"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#usedInME3ValueSet>
                true ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "<p>Due diligence is the review of financial accounts to identify and very specific information and entity account holders, to meet international obligations. There are different due diligence requirements for: AML: Anti-Money Laundering, to combat money laundering and the financing of terrorism; CRS: The Common Reporting Standard, the OEDC initiative to combat global tax evasion [Refer to START Help: Common Reporting Standard (CRS)]; FATCA: Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act, specific United States (US) legislation that aim to reduce tax evasions by US citizens, tax residents, and entities [Refer to START Help: Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act]; The CRS and FATCA leverage established AML procedures and information.<br/>AML: Anti-money laundering - A process that New Zealand Reporting Entities carry out under the Anti-Money Laundering and Countering Financing Terrorism Act 2009. The purpose is to identity and verify the individual customer or the beneficial owner(s) of the entity's customers, and assess the level of money laundering and terrorist financing risk associated with those customers. Note: The applicant must already be a customer or become a customer of a NZ reporting entity in order that CDD can be carried out by the NZ reporting entity. If they are not a customer of a reporting entity, they can become one by choosing to use the service(s) provided by that reporting entity.<br/>There are three types of CDD based on the reporting entities assessment of the risk: Standard, Simplified, Enhanced. There are also three types of verification of the customer's entity the reporting entity may use: Documentary identity verification, Document certification, Electronic identity verification.<br/>Example: under the standard CDD a reporting entity must obtain the following identity information to customers: The person's full name; and The person's date of birth; and If the person is not the customer, the person's relationship to the customer; and The person's address or registered office; and Any information prescribed by regulations. The Reserve Bank has a number of guides setting out the CDD requirements across the customer groups and risk assessment on their website. <br/>Do not refer customers to the Reserve Bank or any other supervising agencies. These supervising agencies do not carry out CDD and are unable to provide any more information. Customers will need to contact a reporting entity directly.<br/></p>"@en ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "Website glossary"@en ;
        <http://smartlogic.com/relationship#Has-retention-class_15_Function_40_RDS_18>
                <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#GDA6-1.2.1_Corporate_Planning_and_Reporting> ;
        sem:guid          "9286da62-f8c9-481d-9c4b-8c0cadf48c67" ;
        skos:broader      <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#FinancialManagement> ;
        skos:definition   "A process that is completed with a New Zealand reporting entity. As part of this process the reporting entity will need to gather information about a customer's identity, and verify a customer's identity to make sure the customer is who they say they are. This process aids the detection, management and mitigation of the risk of money laundering and the financing of terrorism."@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#CustomerDueDiligence/CustomerDueDiligence_en> .

