<http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#NewZealandReportingEntity>
        rdf:type          <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#EntityClassification> ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Instance>
                false ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition>
                "General - START Help"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition0>
                "Website glossary"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "<p>​​A New Zealand reporting entity can be a financial institution, casino or a person declared to be a reporting entity by regulations. They must establish, implement, and maintain a compliance programme that includes internal procedures, policies and controls to: detect money laundering and the financing of terrorism; and manage and mitigate the risk of money laundering and financing of terrorism. Supervising agencies - certain government departments have been tasked with supervising the anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism regime. Below are the government departments and links to lists of the registered reporting entities. The Department of Internal Affairs (DIA) supervises casinos, non-deposit taking lenders, money changes, money remitters, debt collectors, factors, financial leasors, safe deposit box vaults, non-bank credit card providers, stored value card providers and cash transporters, and any other reporting entities not supervised by the Reserve Bank of the Financial Markets Authority. Financial Markets Authority supervises issuers of securities, trustee companies, futures dealers, collective investment schemes, brokers, and financial advisers. The Reserve Bank of New Zealand supervises banks, life insurers, and non-bank deposit takers.<br/>Do not refer customers to the supervising agencies. These supervising agencies do not carry out customer due diligence (CDD) and they are unable to provide any more information. Customers will need to contact a reporting entity directly.<br/>List updating and completeness - These lists are updated periodically. For example the DIA list is updated after the end of each quarter. Some entities listed may not be reporting entities. For example The Reserve Bank list is maintained for other purposes, rather than as a specific list of reporting entities. This means entries on The Reserve Bank list are likely to be reporting entities, but some may not be. Generally no need to check list. These lists have been provided for your information. You don't need to check them unless you are concerned about the status of an organisation which has provided proof of CDD.</p>"@en ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "Website glossary"@en ;
        sem:guid          "43f6f520-4741-4a5c-aa27-b3e894c8169b" ;
        skos:broader      <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#PartyStatus> ;
        skos:definition   "An organisation that provides financial-related services such as banking, foreign exchange, financial advice and life insurance."@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#NewZealandReportingEntity/NewZealandReportingEntity_en> .

<http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#NewZealandReportingEntity/NewZealandReportingEntity_en>
        rdf:type               skosxl:Label ;
        sem:rulebaseBehaviour  sem:RulebaseBehaviourExactPhrase ;
        skosxl:literalForm     "New Zealand reporting entity"@en .

<http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#NewZealandReportingFinancialInstitution>
        rdf:type          <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#EntityClassification> ;
        <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#Abbreviation>
