        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition>
                "IR Strategy 2016 - Policy Agility strand."@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition0>
                "Google AI"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "Policy agility for IR means we: Develop quality advice at the right pace and in the right way; Are joined up, inclusive, and influential; Have a learning approach to design and implementation; and Make the best use of our assets – information and intelligence, human capital, relationships, and deep technical know how."@en ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "Strategy content"@en ;
        sem:guid          "ab9a4feb-158c-406a-aad2-c82842eda43c" ;
        skos:broader      <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#BusinessCapability> ;
        skos:definition   "Policy agility is the capacity of government and organizations to swiftly design, implement, and adapt policies in response to rapid changes in technology, market dynamics, and societal needs. It involves an iterative, outcome-focused, and collaborative approach, contrasted with traditional, static rule-making, to maintain relevance, build public trust, and seize new opportunities in a complex world. "@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#PolicyAgility/PolicyAgility_en> .

