        skos:definition   "A private limited company is a type of business structure that provides limited liability protection to its shareholders, meaning their personal assets are separate from the company's debts. Unlike public companies, a private limited company cannot sell its shares on the stock exchange and is restricted to a maximum of 50 shareholders. This structure is common for small to medium-sized businesses (SMEs) in NZ, offering a distinct legal entity and the advantage of limited personal risk for the owners. "@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#PrivateLimitedCompany/PrivateLimitedCompany_en> .

