<http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#TeWhareTapaWha>
        rdf:type          <http://smartlogic.com/class#Method> ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Instance>
                false ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition0>
                "https://mentalhealth.org.nz/te-whare-tapa-wha"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#IsModelUsedBy>
                <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#MentalHealth> ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "<p><!--StartFragment--><a href=\"https://irnz.sharepoint.com/sites/News/SitePages/Support-your-health-and-wellbeing-in-2023.aspx?web=1\">Supporting your health and wellbeing in 2023 (sharepoint.com)</a><!--EndFragment--><br/></p>"@en ;
        sem:guid          "c4645640-5409-4e85-aff8-e03631f72b8c" ;
        skos:broader      <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#Enterprise-model> ;
        skos:definition   "Te Whare Tapa Whā is a Māori model of health that applies a holistic lens to health and wellbeing. It takes into consideration how different aspects of our physical, mental and emotional, spiritual, and social health interact and can affect overall health and wellbeing. Te Whare Tapa Whā represents foundational aspects of hauora (wellbeing), presented through a wharenui (house).  The foundations need to be strong and stable to ensure the wharenui is safe. If one wall is unstable or damaged this can then affect the strength of other walls or the whole wharenui. This symbolises how different components of wellbeing can affect one another, for example if you are struggling with a physical injury this may also take a toll on your social or emotional health as you are unable to participate in your team sport. Conversely, if your mental or emotional health is suffering you may find it harder to motivate yourself to connect with people or be physically active. The five pillars are: Te Taha Wairua represents spiritual health, the capacity for faith and/or wider communication. Te Taha Tinana represents physical health and the capacity for physical growth and development. Te Taha Whānau represents social/family health, the capacity to belong and take part within social systems. Te Taha Hinengaro represents mental and emotional health, the capacity to communicate, think and feel. Connecting the mind and the body. Whenua is the place where you stand. It is your connection to the land – a source of life, nourishment and wellbeing for everyone."@en ;
        skos:historyNote  "Sir Mason Durie created a holistic model of health to provide a Te Ao Māori perspective. Adopted by the World Health Organisation, this model is also used by non-Māori."@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#TeWhareTapaWha/TeWhareTapaWha_en> .

<http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#TeWhareTapaWha/TeWhareTapaWha_en>
        rdf:type               skosxl:Label ;
        sem:rulebaseBehaviour  sem:RulebaseBehaviourExactPhrase ;
        sem:stemming           sem:StemmingOff ;
        skosxl:literalForm     "Te Whare Tapa Whā"@en .

