<http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Waiata>
        rdf:type          <http://smartlogic.com/class#Topic> ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Instance>
                false ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition>
                "General - START Help. Māori dictionary online"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "<p>Song, chant, psalm.</p><div><p>At formal gatherings waiata are performed to demonstrate one’s affinity, support for and endorsement of their representative and what they've said. Depending on the occasion waiata will, invariably, be chosen on their appropriateness and can sometimes be employed to emphasise a point; invoke support, uplift the reason for the gathering and unify people.​​ IR people should be aware of this component and some staff may be required to learn Te Waiata o te Tari Taake for formal occasions.<br/><br/></p></div>"@en ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Evidence>
                <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Waiata/TeWaiataOTeTariTaake_en> ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "Knowledge Centre"@en ;
        sem:guid          "192fbd2f-74fc-4252-82d8-e82f4ce40d23" ;
        skos:broader      <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Maoritanga> ;
        skos:related      <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#powhiri> ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Waiata/Waiata_en> .

