<http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#AdministrativeAreaType>
        rdf:type          <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Feature> ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#Instance>
                false ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#SourceOfDefinition>
                "http://wiki.gis.com/wiki/index.php/Geographic_feature"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "Geographic features, or geographical formations, are components of a planet that can be referred to (and therefore may show up on maps). There are natural geographic features, abstract geographic features, and on Earth there are also artificial geographic features. Natural geographic features include landforms and ecosystems. Landforms are terrain types and bodies of water. Ecosystems are natural units consisting of all plants, animals and micro-organisms (biotic factors) in an area functioning together with all of the non-living physical (abiotic) factors of the environment. Abstract geographic features include politically designated areas and cartographic features (such as the Equator). Artificial geographic features include settlements and engineered constructs (such as dams, highways, and bridges)."@en ;
        sem:guid          "537d5bce-b587-4505-b878-f95b68505d79" ;
        skos:broader      <http://smartlogic.com/term#112485337176472238565722> ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#AdministrativeAreaType/AdministrativeAreaType_en> .

