        skos:definition   "In New Zealand, a parliamentary question is a formal query by a Member of Parliament (MP) to a Minister in the House of Representatives, either in a written form or during Question Time. It serves as a key method for opposition and other MPs to hold the government accountable, gather information, and publicly scrutinize government activities. Ministers are responsible for answering these questions, often with assistance from their departmental officials. "@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#ParliamentaryQuestion/ParliamentaryQuestion_en> .

