                true ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "<p>Double tax agreements (DTA) are bilateral treaties that remove tax obstacles to cross-border trade and investment and prevent businesses being taxed twice on the resulting income. They also give greater certainty about how cross-boarder investment income is to be taxed, reduce compliance costs and lower tax on some income. DTAs have been negotiated between New Zealand and many other countries or territories to decide which country or territory has the first or sole right to tax specific types of income.<br/>To find out more about DTAs, see the role of double tax agreements or to read the DTAs New Zealand holds with other countries or territories, see the Tax Policy website.</p>"@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#IsExampleOf>
                <http://smartlogic.com/term#143184061161246116921381> ;
