        skos:broader      <http://smartlogic.com/term#492768876094676199672439963971808> ;
        skos:definition   "The Official Information Act 1982 (OIA) is a New Zealand law that allows citizens, permanent residents, and people in New Zealand to request official information held by government agencies. The OIA promotes transparency and accountability by requiring agencies to provide information unless there is a good reason to withhold it. Information such as reports, emails, and internal policies can be requested, though there are some restrictions, such as information protected by lawyer-client privilege. If a request is refused, you can complain to the Office of the Ombudsman, whose recommendations are legally binding. "@en ;
        skosxl:prefLabel  <http://smartlogic.com/label#188291501038379525004105_English> .

