        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#BusinessDefinition>
                "Imputation credits are a portion of the New Zealand income tax paid by a company on its taxable profits, and passed on to shareholders so the dividend is not taxed twice. Before 1989 (when imputation first applied) a company paid income tax on its profits, then the shareholders paid tax again when the profits were distributed in the form of dividends. Sometimes the amount of imputation credits attached will cover all the tax on the dividend. This is called a \"fully imputed dividend\". If the imputation credits attached to your dividend are more than your total tax payable for the year, the difference is excess imputation credits. We cannot refund these excess credits as we do with other credits such as resident withholding tax."@en ;
        <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel-current-#UsesModel>
                <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#Imputation> ;
        <http://example.com/NZ-IRD#Source-of-term>
                "Website glossary; IR website"@en ;
        sem:guid          "7f09105e-72b3-54cb-86be-64657d044016" ;
        skos:broader      <http://smartlogic.com/term#865153476158379467093437320451345> ;
        skos:definition   "A portion of the New Zealand income tax paid by a company on its taxable profits, and passed on to shareholders, so the dividend is not taxed twice."@en ;
        skos:related      <http://example.com/Cleaned-Inland-Revenue-model#ImputationCreditAccount> , <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#AnnualImputationReturn> , <http://example.com/InlandRevenueModel#MutualRecognitionOfImputationCredits> ;
